          "email": "adilger@thelustrecollective.com",
          "date": "2026-11-23 21:56:00.000000000",
          "tz": -360
        },
        "subject": "LU-21306 lmv: keep encrypted op_name for the caller to free",
        "message": "LU-21306 lmv: keep encrypted op_name for the caller to free\n\nIn an encrypted directory, ll_prep_md_op_data() puts the kmalloc'd\nencoded name from ll_setup_filename() into op_name and sets\nMF_OPNAME_KMALLOCED, so ll_finish_md_op_data() can free it.\nlmv_intent_lookup() for MF_GETATTR_BY_FID, and lmv_getattr_name()\nwhen it retries by FID after an OBD_MD_MDS reply, set op_name to\nNULL and never put it back, so the name leaked.  The first path is\ntaken by ll_inode_revalidate(), so each stat() RPC of a file in an\nencrypted directory leaked the encoded name.  The second is taken by\nIOC_MDC_GETFILESTRIPE and IOC_MDC_LOOKUP on a remote entry.\n\nSave op_name and op_namelen at the start of both functions and\nrestore them on every return.  Also restore the name when\nlmv_intent_lookup() retries a getattr by FID, which would otherwise\nassert that op_name is set.\n\nThe name is allocated with kmalloc(), so only kmemleak shows the\nleak and the Lustre memory leak check does not; there is no test.\n\nTest-Parameters: trivial testlist=sanity-sec\nFixes: 4d38566a004f (\"LU-13717 sec: filename encryption\")\nSigned-off-by: Andreas Dilger <adilger@thelustrecollective.com>\nAssisted-by: ClaudeCode:claude-opus-5-5 ltvm llm_code_and_review_tools\nChange-Id: Idf0a418d41e67c0db764d82ab1b0a9f9aa3ebbe5\n"
      },
      "branch": "refs/heads/master"
    }
